ns","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 1 entry","Pages with entries","Terms with Afrikaans translations","Terms with Catalan translations","Terms with Czech translations","Terms with Danish translations","Terms with Dutch translations","Terms with Esperanto translations","Terms with Estonian translations","Terms with Finnish translations","Terms with French translations","Terms with Galician translations","Terms with German translations","Terms with Hungarian translations","Terms with Italian translations","Terms with Latin translations","Terms with Norman translations","Terms with Norwegian translations","Terms with Polish translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Romanian translations","Terms with Russian translations","Terms with Slovak translations","Terms with Spanish translations","Terms with Swedish translations","Terms with Ukrainian translations","Terms with Welsh translations","en:Fictional characters","en:Lewis Carroll"],"glosses":["A fantastical dreaded monster with flaming eyes."],"id":"ef4b6c07-dcaf-4df0-b656-ec0ea6696a64"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"jaboticaba":{"id":"f8fe55ea-3475-401b-9366-6d4eef27a076","word":"jaboticaba","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"Myrciaria cauliflora","tags":["feminine"],"word":"jabuticaba"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"categories":["Entries using missing taxonomic name (species)"],"links":[["fruit","fruit"],["Argentina","Argentina"],["Brazil","Brazil"],["Paraguay","Paraguay"]],"glosses":["The evergreen Brazilian grape tree, Myrciaria cauliflora, a fruit-bearing tree native to Argentina, Brazil, and Paraguay."],"id":"e1465788-416f-43c6-81a6-225ff4a1f502"},{"examples":[{"text":"If you don't have jaboticabas, use red grapes, but make sure they are an old-fashioned seedy, intensely flavoured variety.","ref":"2010, Clare Richards, Tropical Cuisine: Cooking in Clare's Kitchen, Wet Season Press, →ISBN, page 183:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[